  "final_text": "I've built the dashboard and the alert rule, and the rule is enabled. It has already run once successfully and fired no alerts. I checked through the API that all 8 dashboard panels were saved, but I haven't opened the dashboard in the Kibana UI to see them render.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** Each document in `kibana_sample_data_logs` is one web request, timestamped by `timestamp`. The main fields are:\n- `response`: the HTTP status code, stored as text (with a `response.keyword` copy), not as a number.\n- `bytes`: response size.\n- `clientip`: visitor IP.\n- `url`, `request`, `extension`: what was requested.\n- `geo.src` / `geo.dest` / `geo.coordinates`: location.\n- `machine.os`, `agent`, `referer`, `tags`, `host`.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-cb8321ed Web logs overview\"** (id `236fbcb8-fb1d-43c5-b8a2-84e32816ce31`). Its default time range is the last 7 days through tomorrow, so the sample data is in view. It has 8 panels:\n- **Headline numbers:** Total requests, Unique visitors (distinct `clientip`), Average response bytes, and Server errors (5xx).\n- **Requests over time:** a line chart.\n- **Requests by response code:** a pie chart.\n- **Top 10 requested URLs:** a table.\n- **Requests by source country:** a pie chart.\n\nThe \"Server errors (5xx)\" panel uses an ES|QL query that converts `response` to a number before filtering. It's the panel most likely to show an error.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-cb8321ed 5xx spike\"** (id `d7ae2a4d-147a-47d1-a8bb-ca3f1dffd9fd`). It's an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It counts requests in the last 5 minutes whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" and fires when that count is over 10.\n- **No notifications:** it has no actions attached, so a firing alert will only appear in Kibana's alerts view. Add a connector if you want email or Slack messages.\n- **Hits are re-counted:** I set it to count every hit in each 5-minute window even if an earlier run already saw it. That keeps it a true 5-minute count.",
